ing "everyone" believed it.
22:48:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a bit of an exaggeration to say *everyone* thought the Internet was a passing fad in the 1990s—plenty of early adopters, venture capitalists, and tech visionaries saw its world-changing potential. But there was indeed a loud, influential chorus of skepticism that has since become legendary for how wrong it turned out to be. The doubters weren’t just clueless outsiders; they often had rational-sounding arguments rooted in the technological, economic, and social realities of the time. Here’s why so many otherwise smart people dismissed the Internet as hype that would fizzle out. ### 1. The Internet Was Painfully Slow and Clunky For most of the 1990s, going online meant tying up your phone line with a screeching 14.4k or 28.8k modem. Web pages loaded line by line, images took minutes to appear, and video or music streaming was a fantasy. The experience was often frustrating, and critics argued that no ordinary person would tolerate such friction when magazines, TV, and physical stores worked just fine. They saw the Internet as a downgrade from real life, not an upgrade. ### 2. “It’s Just a Nerd Fad” Before the World Wide Web became mainstream (around 1993–1994), the Internet was mostly text-based and dominated by academics, researchers, and hardcore hobbyists. Early skeptics saw it as the next CB radio or ham radio—a niche communication tool for a tiny subculture that would never cross over to the masses. They couldn’t imagine that grandmothers and teenagers would ever want to sit in front of a computer to chat, shop, or read the news. ### 3. No Clear Business Model (and the Dot-Com Crash Seemed to Prove Them Right) Throughout the mid-1990s, companies poured billions into websites with no path to profitability. Many skeptics, armed with traditional business logic, argued that giving away content or services for free was a recipe for disaster. When the dot-com bubble burst in 2000–2001, it looked like the doomsayers had been right all along—pets.com, Webvan, and countless others evaporated, and the dream of a new economy seemed like a collective delusion. ### 4. Established Industries Felt Invulnerable Media moguls, retailers, and publishers compared the Internet to earlier “revolutionary” technologies that flopped, like interactive TV, videotex (e.g., the Minitel in France was actually successful, but many global equivalents failed), and CD-ROM encyclopedias. They believed physical stores, print newspapers, and brick-and-mortar banks had permanent moats. “Why would anyone buy clothes without trying them on?” or “No one will enter a credit card into a computer” were common refrains. ### 5. Walled Gardens Looked More Promising In the early ’90s, services like CompuServe, Prodigy, and America Online (AOL) were curated, easy-to-use “online services” that many thought would win out over the chaotic, unregulated “wild west” of the open Internet. The Internet was compared to a library with no card catalog, while these proprietary networks provided a clean, safe experience. Skeptics assumed the open Internet would remain a messy experiment and that the real money and users would stay in controlled platforms. ### 6. Security and Trust Were Nonexistent Putting personal information online was considered borderline insane. Browser security was immature, scam artists ran wild, and there was no reliable way to verify who you were dealing with. Many believed that commerce would never take off because the average person simply wouldn’t trust the Internet with their credit card number—fears that were entirely justified until encryption and secure sockets (SSL) became standard after 1994–1995. ### 7. It Wasn’t Yet Obvious That Everyone Would Have Something to Say A famous dismissal came from economist Paul Krugman in 1998: “The growth of the Internet will slow drastically… most people have nothing to say to each other! By 2005 or so, it will become clear that the Internet’s impact on the economy has been no greater than the fax machine.” This view reflected a fundamental failure to imagine user-generated content, social media, blogging, or the explosion of niche communities. In the mid-’90s, the Internet was still mostly a read-only medium, and many couldn’t foresee that the “participation” part would become its killer app. ### 8. A Selective Memory for Hype The skeptics weren’t irrational; they’d seen plenty of other “next big things” wither. The 1980s had promised that every home would have a videophone and that virtual reality was just around the corner. The Internet boom felt, to many, like another bout of techno-utopianism that would soon collapse under its own weight. ### The Benefit of Hindsight What the skeptics missed was the exponential curve of Moore’s Law, the rapid spread of broadband in the early 2000s, and the emergence of genuinely useful applications like search engines, e-commerce, and social platforms. They couldn’t predict that the Internet would become deeply integrated into everyday life, mobile phones, and eventually every device around us. Their error wasn’t in pointing out the very real limitations of the mid-1990s Internet—it was in assuming those limitations were permanent. So, no, not everyone thought it would fail. But the loud, confident predictions of its doom are a classic reminder that even the brightest minds can completely misjudge the future when they extrapolate from the present moment.
